function g(topic,nzLink,iDo,weDo,youDo,aos){return{topic,nzLink,iDo,weDo,youDo,aos:aos||[]}}
window.__WWU_TERM_DATA=window.__WWU_TERM_DATA||{};
window.__WWU_TERM_DATA[4]={1:{Monday:g(`Precise verb choice: replacing weak verbs`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): precise, specific verbs carry more meaning than vague general ones; replacing weak verbs (went, said, did, got, moved) with precise alternatives makes writing more vivid and economical.`,{title:`I Do — One Strong Verb Does the Work of Three Weak Ones`,instruction:`Weak verbs like 'went', 'said', 'did', 'moved', and 'got' are placeholder verbs — they tell us almost nothing about HOW something happened. Watch me replace them with precise alternatives that carry the scene.`,example:`She went quickly across the room and said in a loud voice that she was angry. → She stormed across the room and snapped that she was furious.`,demonstration:`'Went quickly' = two words doing a weak job. 'Stormed' = one word that gives direction, speed, AND mood. 'Said in a loud voice that she was angry' = seven words. 'Snapped' = one word that gives volume, emotion, AND manner. Precise verbs make writing leaner and more powerful simultaneously.`,tip:`When you find a weak verb, ask: HOW did they do it? The answer is usually your precise verb (crept, bolted, muttered, slammed).`},{title:`We Do — Upgrade the Verb`,instruction:`Replace the weak verb in each sentence with a more precise one.`,sentences:[`The cat moved along the fence in the dark.`,`He said something under his breath and went out.`,`She did the maths quickly and got the right answer.`],prompt:`What does HOW it happened tell us — is it fast, slow, fearful, angry? What single verb captures that?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Replace every weak verb (went, said, got, did, moved, looked) in each sentence with a precise alternative.`,tasks:[`The dog went to the door and looked at us.`,`She said she was going and got her bag.`,`He moved through the crowd and did his best to get past.`,`✦ Bonus: Take three consecutive sentences from a piece of your own writing. Find every weak verb and upgrade each one.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Precise verb choice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically choosing precise, specific verbs over vague placeholders. Precise verb choice is one of the highest-leverage vocabulary skills for Years 5–6 writing quality.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Economy and precision in word choice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting vocabulary that is economical — doing more work with fewer words. This lesson establishes precise verb choice as the cornerstone vocabulary skill of Term 4.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Colons — introducing lists and elaboration`,`Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the idea in the main clause; the clause before the colon must be a complete sentence.`,{title:`I Do — The Colon Announces What's Coming`,instruction:`A colon says: here comes the detail, the list, or the explanation. The clause before the colon must be a complete sentence. Watch me use it two ways.`,example:`List: She packed everything she needed: a torch, a sleeping bag, and three days' worth of food. | Elaboration: There was only one problem: nobody had thought to bring a map.`,demonstration:`Before each colon I have a complete sentence. After the colon comes either a list (items separated by commas) or an elaboration that explains or expands the main clause idea. I don't put a capital after a colon unless it's a proper noun.`,tip:`Test: can the words before the colon stand alone as a complete sentence? If not, you need more before the colon — or replace it with a comma.`},{title:`We Do — Place the Colon`,instruction:`Add a colon to each sentence in the right place, then check the words before it form a complete sentence.`,sentences:[`There are three rules in this classroom respect others, work hard, and stay curious.`,`The forecast was grim heavy rain, strong winds, and possible flooding.`,`She had one ambition to represent New Zealand at the Olympics.`],prompt:`Where does the list or elaboration begin? Is everything before that point a complete sentence?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Add a colon to each sentence and check it is correctly placed.`,tasks:[`The museum contained many fascinating things ancient fossils, hand-painted maps, and a real Viking helmet.`,`His decision surprised everyone he chose to walk away from the prize.`,`The recipe requires four key ingredients flour, eggs, butter, and sugar.`,`✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences using colons — one introducing a list, one introducing an elaboration.`]},[{code:`Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)`,label:`Colons for lists and elaboration`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): A colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the main clause idea. The clause preceding the colon must be grammatically complete. The colon is a key Phase 2 punctuation mark expected in more sophisticated explanation and persuasive writing.`},{code:`Writing — Punctuation (Practice)`,label:`Applying colons accurately`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect, including the colon for introducing lists and elaboration in explanation and persuasive writing.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Using a colon to introduce and expand an idea`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students build sentences that use a colon to set up a main idea and then expand or clarify it in the second part, creating a sophisticated two-part sentence structure common in formal writing.`,{title:`I Do — Building the Two-Part Sentence`,instruction:`The most powerful use of a colon is in a two-part sentence where part one makes a claim and part two proves, explains, or dramatically reveals it. Watch me build one.`,example:`Claim then proof: The ancient kauri was extraordinary: it had stood for over 2,000 years, surviving storms, drought, and the arrival of humans. | Build-up then reveal: She opened the door and stopped: the room was completely empty.`,demonstration:`In the first example, the colon promises an explanation of 'extraordinary' and delivers one. In the second, the colon creates a pause that makes the reveal more dramatic. Both structures set up reader expectation with part one, then satisfy it with part two.`,tip:`Read part one aloud: does it feel like it's promising something? If yes, the colon is probably right. If part one already feels complete and closed, a full stop may suit better.`},{title:`We Do — Complete the Two-Part Sentence`,instruction:`Complete each sentence starter with a strong part-two after the colon.`,sentences:[`The team had one weakness:`,`The explorer made a startling discovery:`,`There was only one thing left to do:`],prompt:`What does part one promise? Does part two deliver a strong explanation, detail, or reveal?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write the full two-part sentence for each prompt, using a colon to link them.`,tasks:[`Start with: 'The storm had one final surprise'`,`Start with: 'Every great piece of writing shares one quality'`,`Start with: 'She had been preparing for this moment for ten years'`,`✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph opening (3–4 sentences) where the first sentence uses a colon for dramatic elaboration.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Two-part colon sentence structure`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including the two-part colon sentence where the first clause makes a claim or builds anticipation and the second clause provides elaboration or revelation.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Building anticipation and revelation`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including structural choices that create anticipation. The two-part colon sentence is one of the most reliable tools for creating a moment of revelation or emphasis in both narrative and persuasive writing.`}]),Thursday:g(`Editing: upgrading weak verbs throughout a passage`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students systematically identify and replace weak, vague verbs throughout a whole passage, applying the precise verb skill from Monday in a sustained editing context.`,{title:`I Do — The Weak Verb Hunt`,instruction:`Today I'll show you how to edit a whole passage for weak verbs — methodically, not randomly. I highlight every weak verb first, then replace each one before moving on.`,example:`Jake went to school. He looked at his friends and said hello. They all went into class and did their work quietly. The teacher looked up and said they could go.`,demonstration:`Highlight: went (x2), looked (x2), said (x2), did. Replace: Jake trudged / He glanced at his friends and nodded. They filed into class and worked quietly. The teacher glanced up and murmured they were free to go. Now every verb earns its place.`,tip:`Highlight ALL weak verbs before changing any of them — this stops you from improving the first sentence and forgetting the rest.`},{title:`We Do — Highlight Then Replace`,instruction:`Highlight every weak verb in this passage first, then replace them one at a time.`,sentences:[`The soldiers went across the field. They moved carefully because they didn't want to be seen. The leader said they should stop. Everyone stopped and looked around nervously.`],prompt:`How many weak verbs did we find? Which replacement verb best captures the mood — tense, cautious, military?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Edit this passage by replacing every weak verb with a more precise one. Aim to cut word count at the same time.`,tasks:[`The girl went into the kitchen and looked in the fridge. She got out some food and did some cooking. She said to herself that it smelled good. Her mum came in and said she was impressed.`,`✦ Bonus: Count the total words before and after your edit. How many words did you save while making the writing stronger?`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Systematic weak-verb editing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches a systematic, two-step editing strategy — highlight all targets first, then replace — applied specifically to weak verbs across a whole passage.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Verb precision across a sustained passage`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Sustaining precise verb choice across an entire passage — not just a single sentence — is the more demanding and authentic application of this skill at Phase 2.`}])},2:{Monday:g(`Pathetic fallacy: weather and setting reflecting mood`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) & Composing (Practice): pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather reflects or reinforces the emotional mood of a scene or character.`,{title:`I Do — When the World Matches the Feeling`,instruction:`Pathetic fallacy is when the weather or setting mirrors the mood of the scene — the storm that breaks during a crisis, the sunshine that appears at a moment of hope. Watch me use it deliberately.`,example:`Neutral: It was raining as she read the letter. → Pathetic fallacy: The rain hammered against the windows as she read the letter, each drop driving harder as if sharing her despair.`,demonstration:`In the neutral version, rain is just a fact. In the pathetic fallacy version, the rain actively reflects and amplifies her emotional state. I chose 'hammered' and 'driving harder' to match 'despair' — the word choice in the weather description mirrors the emotional register of the scene.`,tip:`The weather shouldn't just be present — it should seem to participate in the mood. Match your weather verbs and adjectives to the emotion of the scene.`},{title:`We Do — Match Weather to Mood`,instruction:`Write a pathetic fallacy description of the weather that reinforces the mood given.`,sentences:[`Mood: dread and foreboding (a character approaches an abandoned house)`,`Mood: joy and relief (a team wins an important match)`,`Mood: loneliness and isolation (someone waits at an empty bus stop)`],prompt:`What weather suits this mood? What verbs and adjectives make the weather seem to share the feeling?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write 1–2 sentences of pathetic fallacy for each scene.`,tasks:[`A character receives unexpected good news on a grey morning.`,`A villain makes their entrance during a hot, still afternoon.`,`Two old friends meet again after many years apart.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4–5 sentences) opening a story, where pathetic fallacy in the first two sentences sets the emotional tone for everything that follows.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Pathetic fallacy`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather is described as reflecting or participating in the emotional atmosphere of a scene. Effective use requires matching weather vocabulary — verbs and adjectives — to the emotional register being conveyed. This is a key narrative craft technique at Year 5–6.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Creating mood through setting`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies, including figurative language and setting description, to create texts that engage the reader. Pathetic fallacy is the deliberate integration of setting and mood — a hallmark of more accomplished narrative writing at Phase 2.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Verb tense consistency in narrative`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge & Practice): narrative writing must maintain a consistent past or present tense throughout; tense shifts within a passage are errors unless used deliberately for effect.`,{title:`I Do — Staying in One Tense`,instruction:`Most narrative writing is written consistently in either the past tense or the present tense. Accidentally switching between them mid-passage is one of the most common errors at Years 5–6. Watch me find and fix tense shifts.`,example:`She walked into the room and sees a shadow by the window. She stops and her heart races. She had looked around but nothing moves.`,demonstration:`'Walked' = past. 'Sees', 'stops', 'races' = present — tense shift. 'Had looked' = past perfect (unnecessary here). Fix: She walked into the room and saw a shadow by the window. She stopped and her heart raced. She looked around but nothing moved. All consistent past tense now.`,tip:`Before editing for tense, decide: is this piece past tense or present tense? Then read through looking ONLY for verbs, checking every one matches your decision.`},{title:`We Do — Find and Fix the Tense Shifts`,instruction:`Find every tense error in this passage and fix it to maintain consistent past tense.`,sentences:[`The door creaks open and Hemi steps inside. He looks around the dark room. A noise comes from upstairs. He grabbed his torch and moves to the stairs.`],prompt:`Which verbs are in the wrong tense? What is the consistent tense this passage should use?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Edit this passage to make it consistently past tense throughout.`,tasks:[`The team arrives at the stadium early. They warm up on the field and the coach gives them a talk. Everyone had felt nervous, but when the whistle blew they play their best game of the season.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of your own in present tense (as if the reader is watching a scene unfold right now) and check it is fully consistent.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Verb tense consistency`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Narrative writing must maintain a consistent verb tense — past or present — throughout a passage. Unintentional tense shifts are grammatical errors that disrupt the reader's experience. Deliberate tense shifts such as past perfect for flashback must be clearly controlled and purposeful.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Editing for tense consistency`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking verb tense. This lesson makes tense consistency an explicit editing skill — deciding the tense first, then reading specifically for verb forms.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Past perfect for flashback`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge & Practice): the past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the main narrative past tense; it is the grammatical tool for writing flashback sequences.`,{title:`I Do — Going Further Back in Time`,instruction:`When we're writing in past tense and want to refer to something that happened even earlier — a flashback — we shift into the past perfect (had + past participle). Watch me build a flashback using it.`,example:`Main narrative (past): She stared at the old photograph. Flashback (past perfect): She had found it tucked inside her grandmother's journal, on the morning everything had changed. Return to narrative: Now, ten years later, she finally understood what it meant.`,demonstration:`The flashback uses 'had found' and 'had changed' — past perfect — to signal these events happened BEFORE 'stared'. When returning to the main narrative, I switch back to simple past. The tense change IS the signal to the reader that we've moved in time.`,tip:`You only need a few past perfect verbs to establish a flashback. Once the reader is clearly in the past, you can drop back to simple past for the body of the flashback.`},{title:`We Do — Build the Flashback`,instruction:`Use past perfect to add a short flashback to each sentence.`,sentences:[`She looked at the trophy on the shelf. (Add a flashback to the day she won it.)`,`He paused at the gate of the old house. (Add a flashback to his childhood there.)`],prompt:`What past perfect verbs open the flashback? How do you signal the return to the main narrative?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a short paragraph (3–5 sentences) for each prompt, using past perfect to signal a flashback and simple past to return.`,tasks:[`A character finds a faded letter and remembers the day it was written.`,`An athlete stands at the start line and recalls her first ever race.`,`✦ Bonus: Take a piece of narrative writing you've done this year and add a flashback sentence using past perfect to deepen the backstory.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Past perfect for flashback`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the point established by the main narrative past tense. It is the grammatical mechanism for writing flashback sequences, allowing writers to move back in time within a past-tense narrative.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Controlling narrative time`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and to use tense deliberately as a structural tool. The past perfect flashback is a significant expansion of students' narrative toolkit — allowing them to manage non-linear storytelling at the sentence-grammar level.`}]),Thursday:g(`Editing for tense consistency`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a tense-consistency editing pass to a multi-paragraph narrative, identifying unintentional tense shifts, distinguishing them from deliberate past perfect flashbacks, and correcting errors systematically.`,{title:`I Do — The Tense Edit Pass`,instruction:`Today I'll model the tense editing pass on a full paragraph — circling every verb, labelling it (simple past / past perfect / present), deciding if shifts are errors or intentional, and fixing the errors.`,example:`The explorers push through the undergrowth. It had been three days since they had lost the path. Now the forest grows darker around them. Last year, Mia had studied this very region — she was their only hope.`,demonstration:`Verbs: 'push' (present — error), 'had been' (past perfect — intentional), 'had lost' (past perfect — intentional), 'grows' (present — error), 'had studied' (past perfect — intentional). Fix: 'pushed', 'grew'. The past perfect verbs stay because they correctly reach back in time.`,tip:`Circle verbs in pencil first, label each one, THEN erase and fix — never edit while circling or you'll lose track of the pattern.`},{title:`We Do — Circle, Label, Fix`,instruction:`Together, circle every verb in this passage, label it (simple past / past perfect / present), and fix any unintentional tense shifts.`,sentences:[`Aroha runs toward the finish line. She had trained for six months for this moment. Her legs ache but she keeps going. Back in January, she had nearly quit. Now she crossed the line first.`],prompt:`Which verbs are errors? Which past perfect verbs are intentional? How do you tell the difference?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Edit this passage for tense consistency. Fix unintentional shifts — keep intentional past perfect verbs.`,tasks:[`The old lighthouse stood at the edge of the cliffs. For years, it had guided ships through the dangerous strait. Now, the light was broken and the keeper has long since gone. He had left one winter when a storm had damaged the lens beyond repair. Today the building sits empty, its windows dark.`,`✦ Bonus: Swap a piece of narrative writing with a partner. Each of you do a tense edit pass and report back what you found.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Tense consistency editing pass`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check accuracy, including verb tense. This lesson makes tense editing a deliberate, methodical pass — circle all verbs, label, then fix — distinguishing between unintentional errors and purposeful past perfect flashbacks.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Distinguishing error from intention in tense`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use tense deliberately for effect, which requires the meta-skill of distinguishing an intentional past perfect construction from an accidental tense shift. This editing lesson develops exactly that judgement within the context of whole-passage revision.`}])},3:{Monday:g(`Emotive language in persuasive writing`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge & Practice): emotive language deliberately targets the reader's feelings — using words with strong emotional connotations — to make a persuasive argument feel more urgent, personal, and compelling.`,{title:`I Do — Making the Reader Feel the Argument`,instruction:`Emotive language doesn't just state facts — it makes the reader feel something about them. Watch me compare a neutral and an emotive version of the same argument.`,example:`Neutral: Some animals are kept in small spaces in zoos. This is not always good for them. → Emotive: Magnificent wild creatures are condemned to pace the same few square metres for their entire lives — robbed of everything nature intended for them.`,demonstration:`'Animals' becomes 'magnificent wild creatures'. 'Small spaces' becomes 'the same few square metres'. 'Not always good' becomes 'condemned', 'robbed' — strong verbs with very negative connotations. The facts are the same — the emotional charge is completely different.`,tip:`Choose emotive words that match your audience's values — what do THEY care about? Tap into those feelings specifically.`},{title:`We Do — Upgrade to Emotive`,instruction:`Rewrite each neutral statement using emotive language that targets the reader's feelings.`,sentences:[`Some children don't have enough food.`,`Old people sometimes feel alone.`,`The native forest is being cut down.`],prompt:`What emotions should the reader feel? Which words or phrases most powerfully trigger those feelings?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Rewrite each statement using emotive language to make it more persuasive.`,tasks:[`Plastic bags are found in the ocean.`,`Animals are tested on in laboratories.`,`Children spend a lot of time on screens.`,`✦ Bonus: Write two versions of a persuasive opening sentence about a cause you care about — one neutral, one emotive. Explain what each version does differently to the reader.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Emotive language in persuasion`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Emotive language deliberately selects words with strong emotional connotations to make persuasive writing feel urgent and personal, targeting the reader's feelings rather than only their reason. Emotive vocabulary choices are matched to the values and concerns of the intended audience.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Selecting words for emotional impact`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically selecting vocabulary for deliberate emotional impact in persuasive writing. This extends Term 3's connotation work into the persuasive writing context.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Conditional sentences`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): conditional sentences express situations and their consequences using if/unless/provided that; students distinguish real (first) from unreal (second) conditionals and use them in persuasive and explanation writing.`,{title:`I Do — What Depends on What`,instruction:`Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (real/possible): If + present simple, will + infinitive. Second conditional (unreal/hypothetical): If + past simple, would + infinitive. Watch me use both.`,example:`First (real): If we reduce plastic use, we will see cleaner oceans within a decade. | Second (unreal): If every child had access to books, the world would be a very different place.`,demonstration:`First conditional = a realistic possibility and its likely result. Second conditional = a hypothetical scenario and its imagined result. In persuasive writing, first conditionals make arguments feel achievable; second conditionals paint a vivid picture of what could be.`,tip:`Check the tense pair: If + present then will (first). If + past then would (second). Mixing them creates an error.`},{title:`We Do — Build the Conditional`,instruction:`Complete each conditional sentence with the correct form, then label it first or second.`,sentences:[`If schools banned single-use plastics, they ___ (reduce) their environmental footprint.`,`If every student reads for 20 minutes a day, their vocabulary ___ (improve).`,`If there were more cycle paths, more people ___ (choose) to cycle to work.`],prompt:`Is this a real/likely scenario (first) or a hypothetical one (second)? What verb form does each need?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a conditional sentence for each persuasive topic, choosing first or second conditional and explaining your choice.`,tasks:[`Topic: reducing food waste.`,`Topic: planting more native trees.`,`Topic: giving students more choice in what they study.`,`✦ Bonus: Write two persuasive sentences about the same topic — one using a first conditional and one using a second conditional. Explain how each creates a different effect on the reader.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Conditional sentences`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (if + present simple, will + infinitive) expresses a real or likely possibility; second conditional (if + past simple, would + infinitive) expresses a hypothetical or unreal scenario. Both are important tools in persuasive writing at Phase 2.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Using conditionals in persuasive writing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of structures, including conditional sentences used deliberately for persuasive effect — first conditional for achievable arguments, second conditional for vivid hypothetical scenarios.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Counter-argument and rebuttal sentence structure`,`Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a sophisticated persuasive text acknowledges the opposing view (counter-argument) and then dismisses or qualifies it (rebuttal); students practise the structural pattern for doing this within a single paragraph.`,{title:`I Do — Showing Both Sides, Winning One`,instruction:`A strong persuasive writer doesn't ignore the other side — they raise it themselves and then knock it down. This shows intellectual honesty and makes the argument more credible. Watch me build a counter-argument and rebuttal.`,example:`Counter: Some people argue that banning plastic bags is inconvenient and costly for businesses. Rebuttal: However, the long-term environmental cost of plastic pollution — estimated at billions of dollars in ocean clean-up — far outweighs any short-term inconvenience.`,demonstration:`I raised the opposing view fairly. Then I used 'however' to pivot and answered it with evidence that outweighs the objection. The rebuttal must be STRONGER than the counter-argument — if it isn't, I haven't won the point.`,tip:`Signal words for counter-argument: 'Some argue that', 'Critics claim'. Signal words for rebuttal: 'However', 'Nevertheless', 'Yet', 'Despite this'.`},{title:`We Do — Build the Counter-Rebuttal Pair`,instruction:`For each position, build a counter-argument sentence and then a rebuttal sentence.`,sentences:[`Position: School uniforms should be compulsory.`,`Position: Students should have phones allowed in class.`],prompt:`What is the strongest objection someone would make? Is the rebuttal stronger than the objection?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a full counter-argument and rebuttal paragraph (3–4 sentences) for the given position.`,tasks:[`Position: All schools should have a vegetarian-only canteen.`,`Position: Homework should be banned for students under 12.`,`✦ Bonus: Take a PEEL paragraph you wrote earlier this term and add a counter-argument and rebuttal to it, making it into a fuller, more sophisticated persuasive paragraph.`]},[{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Counter-argument and rebuttal`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning, including in persuasive writing the acknowledgement and dismissal of opposing views. Counter-argument and rebuttal is an advanced structural expectation for Year 5–6 persuasive writing.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Building credibility through balance`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including in persuasive writing the strategy of conceding then rebutting, which builds the writer's credibility and authority.`}]),Thursday:g(`Editing: strengthening a weak persuasive argument`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students diagnose the specific weaknesses in a persuasive paragraph (vague evidence, missing rebuttal, informal register, weak connectives) and make targeted revisions to strengthen it.`,{title:`I Do — Diagnosing What's Weak`,instruction:`Not all persuasive problems are the same. Watch me read a weak persuasive paragraph and name exactly what's wrong before fixing anything.`,example:`I think everyone should recycle because it's really important. Lots of people already do it. Some people don't bother but they should. Recycling is just a good thing to do.`,demonstration:`Diagnosis: (1) Informal register — 'I think', 'really', 'just'. (2) No specific evidence — 'lots of people' is vague. (3) Counter-rebuttal is present but undeveloped. (4) No persuasive connectives. (5) No PEEL structure. Now I know exactly what to fix, in order of importance.`,tip:`Always diagnose BEFORE revising — if you just start rewriting, you might fix one problem and miss four others.`},{title:`We Do — Diagnose Together`,instruction:`Read this paragraph and produce a diagnosis list before making any changes.`,sentences:[`Kids should be able to choose what they eat at school. The food at school isn't very nice. Some people say healthy food is important but lots of kids don't like it. So schools should let kids choose.`],prompt:`How many problems can we name? Which is the most serious? What is the priority order for fixing them?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Diagnose then rewrite this paragraph to fix all its weaknesses.`,tasks:[`Zoos are bad for animals. Animals don't like being in cages. People argue that zoos help conservation but animals are still unhappy. We should close zoos.`,`✦ Bonus: Diagnose a piece of your own persuasive writing from earlier this term using the same checklist. Write a brief diagnosis, then make the most important fixes.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Diagnosing persuasive weaknesses`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches diagnosis as a deliberate pre-revision step — naming specific weaknesses before beginning revision.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Targeted persuasive revision`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning. This editing lesson consolidates all of Term 4's persuasive skills — register, PEEL, counter-rebuttal, connectives, and emotive language — into a single diagnosis-and-repair process.`}])},4:{Monday:g(`Technical vocabulary in explanation writing`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge & Practice): explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to the topic being explained; students learn to introduce technical terms with definitions and use them consistently.`,{title:`I Do — Using the Right Words for the Job`,instruction:`Explanation writing uses technical terms — the exact, agreed vocabulary of a subject. Watch me introduce a technical term correctly and then use it consistently.`,example:`Weak: Water turns into gas when it gets hot enough. → With technical vocab: When water reaches 100 degrees Celsius, it undergoes evaporation — the process by which liquid water converts into water vapour. This vapour rises and forms part of the water cycle.`,demonstration:`I introduced the technical terms 'evaporation' and 'water cycle' with built-in definitions, then used them consistently instead of replacing them with vaguer words. Technical writing is precise because its audience expects accuracy.`,tip:`Introduce each technical term with a definition the first time, then use it consistently. Don't replace it with a vague synonym — that reduces precision.`},{title:`We Do — Introduce and Use`,instruction:`Upgrade each sentence by introducing a technical term with a definition, then use it in a follow-up sentence.`,sentences:[`The moon's pull makes the ocean water move up and down. (technical term: tidal force / tides)`,`Plants use sunlight to make food. (technical terms: photosynthesis, chlorophyll)`],prompt:`What is the correct technical term? How do we define it for the reader the first time?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Rewrite each sentence by introducing the appropriate technical term and defining it, then write a second sentence using the term correctly.`,tasks:[`Rocks are broken into smaller pieces by weather and water. (key term: erosion/weathering)`,`The heart pumps blood around the body. (key terms: circulation, artery/vein)`,`✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence explanation paragraph about a process you know well, using at least two technical terms correctly introduced with definitions.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Technical vocabulary in explanation writing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to its subject. Technical terms are introduced with clear definitions when first used and then applied consistently — not replaced with vague synonyms — because precision is central to the explanation text type at Phase 2.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Consistent use of technical terms`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including accurate technical vocabulary appropriate to explanation writing. This lesson makes technical term introduction and consistent usage an explicit writing strategy.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Present tense conventions in explanation writing`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense because it describes how things generally work (timeless truth), not what happened on one specific occasion.`,{title:`I Do — Timeless Truth vs. One-Time Event`,instruction:`Explanation writing describes how things GENERALLY work — so it uses the simple present tense (timeless truth), not the past tense (which suggests it only happened once). Watch me compare.`,example:`Past tense (narrative): The volcano erupted and lava flowed down the hillside. → Present tense (explanation): When pressure builds beneath the earth's surface, magma forces its way through cracks and erupts as lava.`,demonstration:`The past tense version sounds like a specific historical event. The present tense version describes the general, repeatable process — which is what explanation writing does. 'Forces', 'erupts' = timeless present = this is how it always works.`,tip:`If your explanation says what happened, switch it to say what happens. That one word change shifts a narrative sentence into an explanation sentence.`},{title:`We Do — Switch to Explanation Tense`,instruction:`Rewrite each past-tense narrative sentence as a present-tense explanation sentence.`,sentences:[`The roots of the plant absorbed water from the soil and carried it to the leaves.`,`The caterpillar made a cocoon and changed into a butterfly inside.`,`The snow melted when the temperature rose above zero.`],prompt:`Does the rewritten sentence describe a general, repeatable process? Does it still make sense in the present tense?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Rewrite each sentence in the timeless present tense appropriate for explanation writing.`,tasks:[`The earthquake shook the buildings and caused cracks in the roads.`,`The bee flew from flower to flower and collected pollen on its legs.`,`The light passed through the prism and split into seven colours.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence explanation of how something natural works, consistently in the timeless present tense.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Present tense in explanation writing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense to convey timeless truth — describing how processes generally work, not what happened on one specific occasion. This distinguishes explanation from narrative and recount text types, which use past tense for specific events.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Text type and tense convention`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): Different text types use different tense conventions: narrative and recount use past tense for specific events; explanation uses simple present for general processes; procedure uses imperative or present tense for instructions. Understanding tense as a text-type signal is a key Phase 2 metalinguistic understanding.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Structuring a full explanation paragraph`,`Writing — Text Structure (Practice): students build a complete, well-structured explanation paragraph with a topic sentence, logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using technical vocabulary, and a concluding link sentence.`,{title:`I Do — The Explanation Paragraph Blueprint`,instruction:`A well-structured explanation paragraph follows a clear pattern: topic sentence (what you're explaining) → cause-and-effect body sentences (the how/why) → concluding link (the outcome or significance). Watch me build one.`,example:`Topic: How does rain form? Topic sentence: Rain forms through a continuous cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation. Body: When the sun heats surface water, evaporation occurs — liquid water converts to water vapour and rises into the atmosphere. As the vapour cools at higher altitudes, it condenses around tiny dust particles, forming clouds. When cloud droplets combine and grow heavy enough, they fall as precipitation. Link: This cycle repeats continuously, driven by solar energy, and is essential to all life on Earth.`,demonstration:`Every sentence does a specific job. Topic sentence names the process and its stages. Body sentences use cause-and-effect connectives and technical terms. Concluding link gives the broader significance.`,tip:`Read your topic sentence and your concluding link together. They should form a logical bracket around the paragraph.`},{title:`We Do — Build Together`,instruction:`Together, build a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given.`,sentences:[`Topic: How does a rainbow form? (Key terms: refraction, spectrum, wavelength)`],prompt:`What goes in the topic sentence? What are the key cause-and-effect steps? What does the concluding link say?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given, using the full blueprint: topic sentence → cause-and-effect body → concluding link.`,tasks:[`Topic: How do volcanoes form? (Key terms: magma, tectonic plates, eruption, lava)`,`✦ Bonus: Write an explanation paragraph on a topic from another subject you're studying at the moment, applying the same blueprint and using correct technical vocabulary.`]},[{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Structuring an explanation paragraph`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning specific to the explanation text type. This lesson gives students an explicit, repeatable blueprint — topic sentence, cause-and-effect body, concluding link — as a transferable cross-curricular writing skill.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Explanation paragraph structure`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): A well-structured explanation paragraph opens with a topic sentence naming the process to be explained, develops through logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using appropriate technical vocabulary, and closes with a linking sentence that states the outcome or broader significance.`}]),Thursday:g(`Editing explanation writing for clarity and accuracy`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply an explanation-specific editing checklist — checking tense, technical vocabulary, cause-and-effect connectives, paragraph structure, and factual accuracy — to revise a weak explanation paragraph.`,{title:`I Do — The Explanation Edit Checklist`,instruction:`Explanation writing has its own specific editing checklist. Watch me apply it to a weak paragraph.`,example:`The water cycle happens when water goes up into the sky. It then came back down as rain. The clouds made from water make rain fall. This is called the water cycle and it happened all the time.`,demonstration:`Checklist: Present tense? — 'came' = past tense error. Technical vocabulary? — 'goes up' should be 'evaporates'. Cause-and-effect connectives? — none used. Topic sentence? — present but vague. Concluding link? — 'happened' = tense error; 'all the time' = informal. Fix each in order.`,tip:`Accuracy matters in explanation writing — if you're not sure a fact or term is correct, flag it for checking rather than leaving an error in.`},{title:`We Do — Apply the Checklist Together`,instruction:`Apply the explanation editing checklist to this paragraph together — check each item in order.`,sentences:[`Photosynthesis is when plants made food. They use the sunlight which hits the leaves. The green stuff in leaves (chlorophyll) catches the sun. Carbon dioxide went in and oxygen comes out. Plants need this for living.`],prompt:`Which checklist item has the most errors? What is the priority order for fixing this paragraph?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Edit this explanation paragraph using the full checklist — tense, technical vocabulary, connectives, structure, and accuracy.`,tasks:[`Erosion happens when rocks and soil got moved by wind and water. Rivers carried rocks downstream and they break into smaller pieces over time. This is why valleys formed and the coastline changes. Erosion happened over millions of years.`,`✦ Bonus: Apply the explanation editing checklist to the paragraph you wrote on Wednesday. What did you find?`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Explanation-specific editing checklist`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact, specifically applying text-type criteria to explanation writing. This lesson gives students a five-point explanation checklist as a transferable cross-curricular editing tool.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Revising explanation structure`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise explanation writing with clear cause-and-effect structure and appropriate language features. The editing checklist consolidates all four days of this week's explanation writing work into one applied revision skill.`}])},5:{Monday:g(`Synonyms for said — precise dialogue tags`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): 'said' is a neutral dialogue tag; replacing it with a precise verb (muttered, snapped, insisted, whispered) tells the reader HOW something was said and adds characterisation without additional description.`,{title:`I Do — Said Tells Us Nothing`,instruction:`'Said' is invisible — readers skip over it. A precise dialogue tag tells the reader the manner, tone, and emotion of speech in one word. Watch me upgrade dialogue by choosing precise tags.`,example:`'I don't know where it is,' she said. → 'I don't know where it is,' she muttered, not meeting his eyes.`,demonstration:`'Muttered' immediately tells us she's evasive, uncertain, perhaps embarrassed. I also added a small beat ('not meeting his eyes') to show rather than tell the emotion further. The combination of precise tag + action beat is the most powerful dialogue technique at this level.`,tip:`Match the tag to the emotion, not just the volume: 'shouted' only tells us it was loud. 'Snarled', 'barked', 'demanded' tell us it was loud AND angry/aggressive.`},{title:`We Do — Choose the Precise Tag`,instruction:`Replace 'said' with the most precise dialogue tag for the context described.`,sentences:[`'Leave me alone,' she said. (emotion: angry, defensive)`,`'I think... maybe... we could try it,' he said. (emotion: uncertain, nervous)`,`'I know exactly where it is,' she said. (emotion: confident, perhaps smug)`],prompt:`What emotion AND manner is the character expressing? Which verb captures both?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Replace 'said' with a precise dialogue tag and add a short action beat to show the emotion further.`,tasks:[`'That's not fair,' she said.`,`'Come on, we'll be fine,' he said.`,`'I didn't do it,' the boy said.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line dialogue between two characters in conflict, using a different precise dialogue tag for each line — no 'said' allowed.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Precise dialogue tags`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including precise dialogue tags that convey manner and emotion rather than simply signalling speech. This is a specific application of the term's precise-verb-choice work to dialogue writing.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Characterisation through dialogue`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including characterisation through dialogue. Precise dialogue tags and action beats together create character voice and emotional subtext without relying on explicit description.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Concessive clauses`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a concessive clause (introduced by although, even though, despite, while, whereas) acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more important one, adding sophistication to both persuasive and analytical writing.`,{title:`I Do — Acknowledging Before Asserting`,instruction:`A concessive clause admits something is true, then asserts a more important contrasting truth. It's the grammar of nuance. Watch me build sentences using it.`,example:`Plain: Some students find homework hard. Many still benefit from it. → Concessive: Although some students find homework challenging, the majority benefit significantly from the practice it provides.`,demonstration:`'Although' introduces the concession (the admitted truth), then the main clause asserts the stronger claim. Other concessive words: 'even though', 'despite', 'while', 'whereas'. Notice that 'despite' takes a noun phrase, not a clause: 'Despite its challenges, homework...' — not 'Despite it is challenging'.`,tip:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a full clause. 'Despite its being difficult' is correct. 'Despite it is difficult' is not.`},{title:`We Do — Build the Concessive`,instruction:`Combine each pair using a concessive clause opener.`,sentences:[`It was raining heavily. The match continued. (use: despite)`,`The new policy has its critics. It has already reduced pollution by 20%. (use: although)`,`The climb was gruelling. The view from the summit made it worthwhile. (use: even though)`],prompt:`Which part is the concession and which is the main assertion? Does 'despite' need a noun or -ing form?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a concessive sentence for each pair using the conjunction given.`,tasks:[`Use 'although': Electric cars are currently expensive / they produce far lower emissions over their lifetime.`,`Use 'despite': The project took three years / the results exceeded all expectations.`,`Use 'whereas': Some students prefer group work / others find individual tasks more productive.`,`✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about the same controversial topic, each using a different concessive conjunction. Do they create subtly different effects?`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Concessive clauses`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A concessive clause, introduced by although, even though, despite, while, or whereas, acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more significant one. 'Despite' takes a noun phrase or gerund, not a full clause. Concessive clauses are a hallmark of sophisticated Phase 2 persuasive and analytical writing.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Nuanced sentence construction`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including concessive constructions that create nuance by balancing two contrasting ideas within a single sentence. This is closely related to the counter-argument work of Week 3.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Building tension with sentence length variation`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) & Composing (Practice): writers deliberately manipulate sentence length to control pace and tension — short sentences slow the reader down and create impact, long sentences sustain flow or build complexity.`,{title:`I Do — Controlling the Reader's Heartbeat`,instruction:`Short sentences make the reader stop, breathe, and feel the weight of a moment. Long sentences carry them forward in a rush of action or detail. The writer's job is to control this deliberately. Watch me use both.`,example:`Long then short: The crowd surged forward, voices rising to a single deafening roar as the scoreboard flickered, counted down, and finally froze on zero. Then: silence.`,demonstration:`The long sentence mimics the building rush of noise and action. 'Then: silence.' — two words, the colon, the full stop — brings everything to a dead halt. The contrast makes both sentences more powerful than if the whole passage were the same length.`,tip:`For a sudden shock or revelation, make it the shortest sentence in the paragraph. The contrast with the sentences around it does the work.`},{title:`We Do — Design the Tension`,instruction:`Rewrite this passage by varying the sentence lengths to build tension toward the last sentence.`,sentences:[`She walked to the door. She opened it. There was a figure standing outside. She screamed.`],prompt:`Which sentences should be long (building dread)? Which single sentence should be the shortest (impact point)?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a tense paragraph (5–7 sentences) for each scenario, deliberately varying sentence length to build toward a single short impact sentence at the end.`,tasks:[`Scenario: a player takes the last penalty in a shootout.`,`Scenario: a character reaches the top of a mountain and sees what's on the other side.`,`✦ Bonus: Find a tense moment in a book you're reading. Count the sentence lengths. Does the author use this technique? Copy the pattern into your own writing about a different scenario.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Sentence length variation for tension`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, specifically using deliberate sentence-length contrast to control pace and tension in narrative writing. This formalises the intuitive understanding of rhythm developed in Term 3 into a conscious narrative tool.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Controlling narrative pace and tension`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including deliberate sentence-length manipulation for pacing and tension. This is one of the highest-level narrative craft skills at Phase 2.`}]),Thursday:g(`Editing narrative pacing and tension`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a pacing-and-tension editing lens to a narrative passage — identifying where pace is too flat, adding sentence-length variation, short impact sentences, and precise verbs to heighten tension.`,{title:`I Do — Reading for Pace`,instruction:`When editing for pacing, I read the passage aloud at its current rhythm and ask: where does it drag? Where does it rush past something important? Watch me diagnose and fix a flat passage.`,example:`He walked to the edge. He looked down. It was very high. He felt scared. He stepped back.`,demonstration:`Every sentence is the same length — the rhythm is completely flat. Fix: He crept to the edge, his breath shallow and quick. He looked down. Far below, the river glittered, cold and indifferent. He was so high. His foot found the edge — and he stepped back. Now there's a build, a reveal, a moment of dread, an impact, and a release.`,tip:`Read the passage aloud. If every sentence takes the same breath to say — vary at least one.`},{title:`We Do — Diagnose and Fix`,instruction:`Read this passage aloud. Identify where the pacing is flat, then rewrite it with deliberate sentence-length variation.`,sentences:[`The storm was getting worse. The boat rocked from side to side. A wave hit the deck. Everyone held on. Another wave came. The boat tilted dangerously.`],prompt:`Where should pace slow down to build dread? Where should a single short sentence hit like a punch?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Edit this passage for pacing and tension using sentence-length variation, precise verbs, and one short impact sentence.`,tasks:[`The lion moved through the grass. It was looking at the zebra. The zebra didn't see it. The lion got closer. Then it ran at the zebra.`,`✦ Bonus: Take the most tense moment from a piece of your own narrative writing. Read it aloud, identify the flattest part, and revise specifically for sentence-length variation and precise verb choice.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Editing for pacing and tension`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches reading aloud as a pacing diagnostic tool and then applies sentence-length variation and precise verbs as the specific revision tools.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Pacing as a craft decision`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including managing narrative pacing as a deliberate craft choice. This editing lesson applies Wednesday's sentence-length technique to a real revision context.`}])},6:{Monday:g(`Cohesive substitution — avoiding repetition`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): cohesive substitution replaces repeated nouns or noun phrases with synonyms, pronouns, or general-reference nouns to create flow without monotony, while maintaining clarity about who or what is being referred to.`,{title:`I Do — Saying the Same Thing a Different Way`,instruction:`Repeating the same noun in every sentence is monotonous. But replacing it carelessly can confuse the reader. Watch me substitute carefully.`,example:`Repetitive: The kiwi is a flightless bird. The kiwi is nocturnal. The kiwi is endangered. The kiwi can only be found in New Zealand. → Substituted: The kiwi is a flightless, nocturnal bird. This remarkable creature is endangered and can only be found in New Zealand — our unique national symbol.`,demonstration:`'The kiwi' becomes 'this remarkable creature' (adds description) and then 'our unique national symbol' (adds perspective). Both substitutions keep it clear we're still talking about the kiwi, while adding new information.`,tip:`Never substitute in a way that could confuse the reader about who or what you mean. If the substitution could refer to something else, keep the noun.`},{title:`We Do — Substitute Without Confusing`,instruction:`Rewrite this passage by substituting the repeated noun at least twice — without confusing the reader.`,sentences:[`The kauri tree is one of New Zealand's most ancient trees. The kauri tree can live for thousands of years. The kauri tree was heavily logged in the 19th century. The kauri tree is now a protected species.`],prompt:`What synonyms or pronoun references could replace 'the kauri tree'? Does each substitution still clearly refer to the kauri?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Rewrite each passage using cohesive substitution to reduce repetition without losing clarity.`,tasks:[`Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth. Elephants live in family groups. Elephants have excellent memories. Elephants are threatened by habitat loss and poaching.`,`✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph of your own writing and highlight every repeated noun. Try at least two substitutions — then check that a reader could still follow clearly who or what you're talking about.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Cohesive substitution`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including using synonyms, pronouns, and reference nouns as cohesive substitution devices to reduce repetition while maintaining textual clarity. This is an advanced vocabulary and cohesion skill at Phase 2.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Cohesion through substitution`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use cohesive devices — including pronouns and reference words — to link ideas within and across sentences. This lesson extends Term 3's cohesion work beyond connectives into the more subtle substitution devices that distinguish mature Phase 2 writing.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Relative clauses for embedded definitions`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a relative clause (introduced by which, who, where, whose) can embed a definition or explanation directly inside a sentence, avoiding a clunky separate definition sentence — a key technique in explanation and formal writing.`,{title:`I Do — Defining Without Interrupting`,instruction:`Instead of writing a separate definition sentence, I can embed the definition inside the main sentence using a relative clause. Watch me do it.`,example:`Separate: Photosynthesis is a process. It converts sunlight into energy. Plants use it. → Embedded: Photosynthesis, which converts sunlight into chemical energy, is the process that sustains almost all plant life on Earth.`,demonstration:`The relative clause 'which converts sunlight into chemical energy' is embedded between commas, defining the term right where it appears. The reader gets the definition without the flow being interrupted by a separate sentence.`,tip:`If the relative clause is essential to identifying WHICH thing you mean, don't use commas. If it's adding extra information about something already identified, use commas around it.`},{title:`We Do — Embed the Definition`,instruction:`Combine each pair into one sentence using a relative clause to embed the definition.`,sentences:[`Erosion is a process. It gradually wears away rock and soil.`,`Te Tiriti o Waitangi is a document. It was signed in 1840 between Maori chiefs and the Crown.`,`The ozone layer is a region of Earth's atmosphere. It absorbs most of the sun's ultraviolet radiation.`],prompt:`Does the relative clause add extra information (commas) or identify which one (no commas)?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Rewrite each pair as a single sentence, embedding the definition with a relative clause.`,tasks:[`Metamorphosis is a process. It transforms a caterpillar into a butterfly.`,`New Zealand is a country. It was the first in the world to grant women the right to vote.`,`A neuron is a cell. It transmits electrical signals through the nervous system.`,`✦ Bonus: Find a technical term in your reading or class notes. Write a sentence that introduces and defines it using an embedded relative clause.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Relative clauses for embedded definitions`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including using relative clauses to embed definitions or elaborations directly within a sentence — a key tool for explanation and formal writing. This consolidates relative clause knowledge from earlier terms into a specific, functional application.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Technical vocabulary integration`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including integrating technical terms with their definitions smoothly and economically within explanation sentences. The embedded relative clause definition is the most sophisticated tool for this at Phase 2.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Topic sentences and paragraph-closing sentences`,`Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a well-constructed paragraph opens with a topic sentence that states the main idea and closes with a sentence that clinches or links that idea; students distinguish strong from weak topic sentences and write effective closers.`,{title:`I Do — Opening and Closing a Paragraph Cleanly`,instruction:`The topic sentence is a promise to the reader about what this paragraph will deliver. The closing sentence clinches that promise or links to the next paragraph. Watch me write both.`,example:`Weak topic sentence: 'This paragraph is about recycling.' → Strong: 'Recycling reduces the pressure on landfill sites while conserving the natural resources needed to produce new materials.' Weak closer: 'So that is why recycling is good.' → Strong: 'For these reasons, recycling is not merely a personal choice — it is a shared environmental responsibility.'`,demonstration:`The strong topic sentence makes a specific claim that the paragraph can prove. The strong closer doesn't just repeat the topic — it elevates it, leaving the reader with a stronger impression.`,tip:`Test your topic sentence: could the reader predict what your paragraph will contain? Test your closer: does it add something, or just repeat the topic sentence?`},{title:`We Do — Improve the Topic and Closer`,instruction:`Improve the weak topic sentence and closer for this paragraph.`,sentences:[`Weak topic: 'New Zealand has a lot of native birds.' [body: kiwi, kakapo, takahe details] Weak closer: 'So New Zealand native birds are quite unique.'`],prompt:`What specific claim could the topic sentence make? What does the closer need to add — elevation, significance, or a link?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a strong topic sentence and closing sentence for each paragraph body given.`,tasks:[`Body: sentences about how exercise releases endorphins, reduces stress hormones, and improves sleep quality.`,`Body: sentences about how deforestation reduces biodiversity, increases carbon emissions, and leads to soil erosion.`,`✦ Bonus: Take any paragraph from a piece of your own writing. Rewrite its topic sentence to make a more specific claim, and rewrite its closing sentence to elevate or link rather than just repeat.`]},[{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Topic and closing sentences`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea established by a strong topic sentence, and to close paragraphs with a sentence that clinches or links — not merely repeats — the main idea. Strong topic and closing sentences are hallmarks of well-controlled Phase 2 writing.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Paragraph-level composition`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including at the paragraph level — opening with a specific, testable claim and closing with an elevated or linking sentence.`}]),Thursday:g(`Adapting a piece for a different audience`,`Writing — Composing (Practice) & Vocabulary (Practice): students revise a piece of writing for a different audience, changing vocabulary, register, examples, and level of detail to suit the new reader without changing the core information or argument.`,{title:`I Do — Same Message, Different Reader`,instruction:`The same information can be written very differently depending on the audience. Watch me adapt a paragraph about recycling from a formal audience to a young audience.`,example:`Formal: Recycling reduces landfill pressure and conserves finite natural resources, contributing to a circular economy. | Young audience: When we recycle, we help keep rubbish out of enormous rubbish dumps called landfills, and we save natural materials so they can be used again.`,demonstration:`Same facts, completely different vocabulary, sentence structure, and examples. Neither version is wrong — they're both right for their audience.`,tip:`Ask three questions before adapting: (1) How much does this audience already know? (2) What vocabulary will they understand? (3) What examples will feel relevant to their lives?`},{title:`We Do — Adapt Together`,instruction:`Adapt this paragraph — written for adults — for an audience of Year 4 students.`,sentences:[`New Zealand's native biodiversity is under significant threat from introduced predators, particularly rats, stoats, and possums, which compete with or prey directly upon endemic species.`],prompt:`What vocabulary needs changing? What background knowledge can we assume? What everyday examples could replace the technical ones?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Adapt each passage for the new audience given.`,tasks:[`Original (for Year 6): 'Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert solar energy into glucose using chlorophyll.' → Adapt for Year 2.`,`Original (for Year 2): 'Leaves are green because of a special ingredient called chlorophyll.' → Adapt for an adult science magazine.`,`✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own persuasive or explanation writing and adapt it for two different audiences — your school principal, and a Year 3 student. What changes between the two versions?`]},[{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Audience adaptation`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies appropriate to audience and purpose, including adapting existing writing for a new reader by changing vocabulary, register, sentence structure, and examples while preserving the core content. Audience adaptation is a key Phase 2 composing skill with direct cross-curricular application.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Register and vocabulary for audience`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience. This lesson applies the full year's vocabulary and register work to a genuine revision task — rewriting for a different audience — making register not just a label but an active writing decision.`}])},7:{Monday:g(`Hyperbole for effect`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally.`,{title:`I Do — The Art of Enormous Exaggeration`,instruction:`Hyperbole is deliberate over-the-top exaggeration for effect. Unlike lying, the reader KNOWS it's exaggerated — that's the point. Watch me use it for comic and dramatic effect.`,example:`Comic: I've told you a million times to clean your room. | Dramatic: The silence in the hall was so complete you could have heard a heartbeat three streets away.`,demonstration:`'A million times' is obviously untrue — its comic effect comes from the scale of the exaggeration. 'Heard a heartbeat three streets away' is physically impossible — its dramatic effect comes from the image of extreme, unnatural stillness.`,tip:`Hyperbole works best when it's CLEARLY impossible — the more obviously exaggerated, the better. Mild exaggeration just sounds like an inaccuracy.`},{title:`We Do — Build the Hyperbole`,instruction:`Write a hyperbolic sentence for each situation, making the exaggeration as vivid and obviously impossible as possible.`,sentences:[`The bag was very heavy.`,`The test was very long.`,`He was extremely hungry.`],prompt:`Is the exaggeration obvious enough to be clearly hyperbole? Does it create a vivid image?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a hyperbolic sentence for each prompt.`,tasks:[`She ran very fast.`,`The crowd was enormous.`,`It was a very boring speech.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a short comic paragraph (3–4 sentences) about a small everyday annoyance using at least two hyperboles. Aim to make the reader laugh.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Hyperbole`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally by the reader. Its effectiveness depends on the scale of the exaggeration being recognisably impossible. This completes the year's range of figurative language devices.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Comic and dramatic voice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including humour as a deliberate compositional choice. Hyperbole is one of the primary tools for creating comic voice in writing.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Sentence fragments for deliberate effect`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a sentence fragment lacks a subject, verb, or complete thought; while fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing for pace, emphasis, and dramatic effect.`,{title:`I Do — Breaking the Rules on Purpose`,instruction:`A fragment is technically incomplete — but skilled writers use them deliberately in narrative for impact. The key is: it must be clearly intentional, not accidental. Watch me use a fragment deliberately.`,example:`Complete sentences: He reached the top of the hill and looked out at the view. He had never seen anything like it. → With deliberate fragment: He reached the top of the hill and looked out. Nothing could have prepared him. Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.`,demonstration:`'Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.' — three fragments in a row. They create a staccato, breathless effect, mimicking the gasp of someone overwhelmed by a view. The incompleteness IS the effect.`,tip:`Only use deliberate fragments in creative/narrative writing — NEVER in formal, persuasive, or explanation writing. And always be able to explain why the fragment is there.`},{title:`We Do — Add a Deliberate Fragment`,instruction:`Add one or two deliberate fragments to heighten the impact of each passage.`,sentences:[`She opened the box. Inside was a letter she had been waiting for.`,`The final whistle blew. The scoreboard showed the result.`],prompt:`Where would a single-word or short fragment create the most impact? Is the fragment placement clearly intentional?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Add deliberate fragments to each narrative moment to heighten the effect. Mark each fragment with [F] so it's clear it's intentional.`,tasks:[`He stepped off the plane into the cold air of a new country.`,`She looked at the test results for a long time.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph describing an overwhelming moment that uses at least three deliberate fragments for effect.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Deliberate sentence fragments`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A sentence fragment is a grammatically incomplete sentence lacking a subject, verb, or complete thought. While fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing to create pace, emphasis, or a staccato dramatic effect. Deliberate fragments must be clearly intentional and contextually justified.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Style and craft in narrative voice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader. Understanding when and why to break a grammar rule — and being able to justify the choice — is a hallmark of the most sophisticated writing at Phase 2 and into Year 7.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Opening hooks for different text types`,`Writing — Composing (Practice): an effective opening hook immediately engages the reader; different text types use different hook strategies — narrative (in-scene, sensory), persuasive (bold claim, statistic, rhetorical question), explanation (surprising fact, question).`,{title:`I Do — The First Sentence Does the Heavy Lifting`,instruction:`The opening sentence determines whether the reader continues. Watch me write strong hooks for three different text types.`,example:`Narrative hook (in-scene): The smell of smoke reached her before the alarm did. | Persuasive hook (bold claim): Every day, the equivalent of 2,000 trees' worth of paper is wasted in New Zealand's schools. | Explanation hook (surprising fact): Despite its name, a starfish is not actually a fish — and cannot survive outside saltwater for more than a few minutes.`,demonstration:`Each hook uses a different strategy: narrative puts the reader immediately into a sensory moment. Persuasive opens with a specific, credible statistic. Explanation opens with a counterintuitive fact that creates curiosity.`,tip:`The hook's job is to make the reader ask a question they want answered. What question does each of your hooks make the reader want to answer?`},{title:`We Do — Write the Hook`,instruction:`Write a strong opening hook for each text type and topic.`,sentences:[`Narrative: a character who wakes up in an unfamiliar place.`,`Persuasive: why zoos should be redesigned as conservation sanctuaries.`,`Explanation: how the human eye works.`],prompt:`Which strategy suits each text type? Does your hook make the reader want to read the next sentence?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write two different opening hooks for each topic — one using a narrative strategy, one using a persuasive or explanation strategy.`,tasks:[`Topic: the ocean.`,`Topic: learning a new skill.`,`✦ Bonus: Go back to a piece of writing you completed earlier this term. Rewrite its opening sentence using a different hook strategy — does the new hook work better?`]},[{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Opening hooks by text type`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting an appropriate opening hook strategy matched to the text type and purpose. Narrative hooks use sensory/in-scene openings; persuasive hooks use bold claims, statistics, or rhetorical questions; explanation hooks use surprising or counterintuitive facts.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Text-type appropriate openings`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas with openings appropriate to the text type, including hooks that immediately signal purpose and engage the reader.`}]),Thursday:g(`Structural edit — whole-text level`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): a structural edit evaluates the organisation of a complete piece of writing — checking that the opening hooks, paragraphs are in the right order, transitions are clear, and the ending resolves what the beginning promised.`,{title:`I Do — Editing the Shape, Not the Sentences`,instruction:`A structural edit ignores spelling and grammar — it only asks: does the whole piece have the right shape? Watch me apply a structural checklist to a piece plan.`,example:`Structural checklist: Does the opening hook immediately engage? Are paragraphs in the most logical/effective order? Is there a clear climax or argument progression? Do transitions between paragraphs feel inevitable? Does the ending resolve or reflect back on what the opening promised?`,demonstration:`I apply this top-down, not bottom-up. A structural problem (paragraph 3 should be paragraph 1) is more important than a word choice issue — fix structure first, always.`,tip:`Print or copy your writing and cut it into paragraphs. Can you rearrange them into a better order? If one paragraph could be removed without the reader noticing — it probably should be.`},{title:`We Do — Structural Diagnosis`,instruction:`Apply the structural checklist to this piece outline together. What needs moving or improving?`,sentences:[`1. Conclusion: recycling is important. 2. Evidence: statistics about landfill. 3. Counter-argument. 4. Hook: what if every piece of rubbish you ever threw away was still here? 5. Main argument. 6. Rebuttal.`],prompt:`Is this in the right order? Where does the hook belong? What should the conclusion say rather than just 'recycling is important'?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Apply the structural checklist to a full piece of your own writing. List at least two structural improvements, then make the most important one.`,tasks:[`Does the opening hook immediately engage?`,`Are paragraphs in the most effective order?`,`Is there a clear progression/climax?`,`Do transitions feel inevitable?`,`Does the ending deliver on the opening's promise?`,`✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and do a structural edit of their piece. Tell them: what the shape is, what is working, one specific structural change you'd recommend.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Structural editing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson distinguishes structural editing (whole-text organisation) from sentence-level editing — establishing that structure is always revised first, before sentence-level polish.`},{code:`Writing — Text Structure (Practice)`,label:`Whole-text organisation`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with clear whole-text shape — appropriate opening, logical progression, and satisfying ending. The structural edit is the culminating text-structure skill of the year, applied independently to a complete piece.`}])},8:{Monday:g(`Vocabulary of concession`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge & Practice): concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge a valid opposing point while maintaining their own position, adding nuance and credibility to argument.`,{title:`I Do — Conceding Without Surrendering`,instruction:`Concessive vocabulary lets you admit something is true (concede a point) while keeping your main argument firmly in place. Watch me use several different concessive words to do this.`,example:`Admittedly, electric cars are currently more expensive to purchase. Nevertheless, their lower running costs and environmental benefits make them the more sensible long-term investment.`,demonstration:`'Admittedly' signals: I acknowledge this objection. 'Nevertheless' signals: but here's why my argument still holds. Other pairs: 'Granted... even so'; 'It is true that... yet'; 'That said...'. The concession makes the writer seem balanced and credible.`,tip:`The concessive word and the rebuttal word are a pair — don't use one without the other, or your argument sounds unfinished.`},{title:`We Do — Choose the Right Concessive`,instruction:`Add an appropriate concessive phrase and a rebuttal to each situation.`,sentences:[`Some people argue that homework causes too much stress. [Concede + rebut]`,`Visiting zoos may be educational for some children. [Concede + rebut]`,`Online learning is undeniably more flexible than traditional schooling. [Rebut this concession]`],prompt:`Which concessive word fits best — admittedly, granted, that said, even so? Does the rebuttal actually outweigh the concession?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a concede-and-rebut sentence pair for each topic, using a different concessive word each time.`,tasks:[`Topic: whether competitive sport is good for children.`,`Topic: whether social media should be available in schools.`,`Topic: whether animals should be kept as pets.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph on a topic of your choice that uses two different concessive phrases — one near the beginning and one near the end — to build a nuanced, credible argument.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Vocabulary of concession`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge valid opposing points while maintaining their own argument. They are always paired with a rebuttal signal word that reasserts the writer's position. This vocabulary extends Term 3's connective work and Term 4 Week 3's counter-argument structure into a more sophisticated one-sentence technique.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Nuance in persuasive vocabulary`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including the vocabulary of concession in persuasive writing — words that signal intellectual balance and credibility rather than one-sided assertion.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Review: subordination vs coordination across a paragraph`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge & Practice): students review and apply the distinction between coordinating and subordinating conjunctions across a full paragraph, ensuring controlled variety of simple, compound, and complex sentences.`,{title:`I Do — The Full Mix in One Paragraph`,instruction:`Today we review the year's conjunction work and check we can mix simple, compound, and complex sentences fluently in one paragraph. Watch me read a paragraph and label each sentence type.`,example:`The hawk circled silently. [simple] It rode the thermal higher and higher, and the valley fell away beneath it. [compound] Although the air was still warm, the bird sensed the approaching evening. [complex] It folded its wings and dropped. [compound-simple]`,demonstration:`Reading through: every sentence is grammatically correct. No two consecutive sentences are the same type. Short simple sentences contrast with longer compound and complex ones. The variety creates rhythm and sustains the reader's interest.`,tip:`Write a paragraph, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. If you have three in a row of the same type — vary the next one.`},{title:`We Do — Label and Improve`,instruction:`Label each sentence in this paragraph S / Co / Cx and identify where the variety breaks down.`,sentences:[`The boat left the harbour. The sea was calm and the wind was light. They sailed north. The islands came into view. They anchored and they went ashore. They found the beach deserted.`],prompt:`Where is the variety lacking? Which sentences could be combined or restructured to create better rhythm?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences on the topic given, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. Ensure no three consecutive sentences share the same type.`,tasks:[`Topic: arriving at a new place for the first time.`,`✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own writing. Label every sentence S / Co / Cx. If any type is missing — add or convert a sentence to include it.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Sentence variety: S/Co/Cx review`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the year's sentence-structure strand — simple, compound, and complex sentences created through coordinating and subordinating conjunctions — requiring students to apply all three types within a single paragraph with controlled variety. This is a comprehensive review of the year's grammar in context.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Controlled sentence variety in a paragraph`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity. This review lesson requires students to demonstrate that sentence variety is not accidental but planned — labelling sentence types and deliberately ensuring variety across a paragraph, as they will be expected to do independently in Year 7.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Combining techniques — planning before drafting`,`Writing — Composing (Practice) & Writing Process (Knowledge): students plan a short piece of writing by deliberately selecting which techniques to deploy (vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation) BEFORE drafting, then write to their plan.`,{title:`I Do — The Technique Plan`,instruction:`The most controlled, effective writing comes from planning which techniques to use BEFORE you start, not hoping they appear. Watch me make a technique plan for a short piece.`,example:`Topic: the moment before a performance. Plan: Hook — in-scene sensory opening. Vocabulary — precise verbs, pathetic fallacy. Sentences — -ing opener, long-then-short contrast for tension. Punctuation — colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for effect. Register — narrative, first person.`,demonstration:`Having this plan means every choice I make while drafting is purposeful. I'm not searching for a technique — I'm executing a plan. This is how published writers work.`,tip:`Aim for 4–5 techniques in your plan. More than that and the piece feels forced — every sentence feels like it's showing off rather than telling a story.`},{title:`We Do — Plan Together`,instruction:`Together, build a technique plan for this topic before writing a single sentence.`,sentences:[`Topic: the last five minutes of a championship match.`],prompt:`What hook strategy? Which vocabulary techniques? Sentence structures for tension? Punctuation for effect? What register and person?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Build a technique plan, then write a paragraph (5–7 sentences) to your plan. Label each technique you used.`,tasks:[`Topic: discovering something unexpected in an old building.`,`✦ Bonus: Write the same topic twice — once with a plan, once without. Which version is more controlled and effective? What does the planning add?`]},[{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Planning technique use before drafting`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including planning specific techniques before drafting — selecting vocabulary devices, sentence structures, and punctuation choices in advance rather than improvising. Planning-before-drafting is a key indicator of writing maturity at Phase 2.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)`,label:`Pre-drafting planning strategies`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan their use of techniques — vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation choices, and register — before beginning to draft, rather than hoping these features will appear incidentally. This lesson formalises the planning stage of the writing process as an explicit, teachable skill.`}]),Thursday:g(`Peer editing for voice and style`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a voice-and-style checklist when peer editing, evaluating whether a partner's writing has a consistent, distinctive voice and whether stylistic choices are deliberate and effective.`,{title:`I Do — Reading for Voice`,instruction:`Voice is the sense that a real person is behind the words — consistent in register, distinctive in word choice, deliberate in style. Watch me read two versions of the same passage and identify which has voice and why.`,example:`Version A: The old man sat in the chair by the window. He looked at the street. It was busy. Version B: Every morning, he claimed his chair by the window like a king ascending a throne — watching the street below with the patient authority of someone who had earned the right to simply observe.`,demonstration:`Version A is accurate but voiceless. Version B has consistent register (slightly elevated, respectful), metaphor (king/throne), precise verb ('claimed'), and rhythm. The style feels like a choice, not a default.`,tip:`Ask: if I removed the name from this piece, would I know who wrote it? If yes, there's voice. If it could have been written by anyone — it probably needs more.`},{title:`We Do — Voice Checklist`,instruction:`Apply the voice checklist to this passage together.`,sentences:[`The forest was dark and quiet. We walked through it. There were lots of trees and bushes. We heard some birds. It was a nice experience.`],prompt:`What voice checklist items are missing? Register? Distinctive word choice? Figurative language? Sentence variety?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Swap a piece of writing with a partner. Apply the voice-and-style checklist and write specific feedback — one strength and one specific suggestion.`,tasks:[`Is register consistent throughout?`,`Are word choices distinctive — or could anyone have written this?`,`Is figurative language present and purposeful — or absent/cliched?`,`Is sentence variety clearly deliberate — or flat?`,`Does the piece sound like a real, specific writer?`,`✦ Bonus: Using your partner's feedback, revise the weakest section of your own piece for voice — aim to make it clearly, identifiably yours.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Peer editing for voice and style`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work, including receiving and giving feedback specifically on voice and style — the most subjective but also most important dimension of writing quality at Phase 2.`},{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Developing a distinctive writing voice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including developing a consistent, distinctive writing voice through deliberate choices of register, vocabulary, figurative language, and sentence structure. This is the culminating compositional skill of the year.`}])},9:{Monday:g(`Year vocabulary review — choosing your best tool`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge & Practice): students review the full year's vocabulary toolkit and practise selecting the best tool for a given writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.`,{title:`I Do — The Full Toolkit`,instruction:`This year we built a vocabulary toolkit. Today, the challenge is not 'can you use this device' but 'which device should you use here, and why?' Watch me make that choice explicitly.`,example:`Prompt: describe a wildfire approaching a town. Options: simile? metaphor? personification? precise verbs? pathetic fallacy?`,demonstration:`Personification — the fire devoured the hillside — works because fire as a predator creates dread. Pathetic fallacy — if the sky turns blood-red — sets mood before the fire arrives. Precise verbs — the fire crept, then surged, then consumed — gives pace and progression. Best combination: start with pathetic fallacy for mood, then precise verbs for pace, then one strong personification at the peak.`,tip:`The question is never 'which device do I know?' It's always 'which device does this moment need?'`},{title:`We Do — Toolkit Decision`,instruction:`For each writing prompt, decide which vocabulary technique(s) would be most effective and explain why — before writing any sentences.`,sentences:[`Persuasive: argue for protecting New Zealand's native forests.`,`Explanation: describe how a glacier forms.`,`Narrative: the moment a character realises they are lost.`],prompt:`Which vocabulary device(s) suit each text type and purpose? Why are some devices better fits than others here?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`For each prompt, choose your two best vocabulary tools, explain your choice, and write two sentences using them.`,tasks:[`A character watches their home disappear as a ship leaves port.`,`An explanation of why coral reefs are important ecosystems.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph for one of the prompts above, using at least four different vocabulary techniques deliberately. Label each one in the margin.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Year vocabulary toolkit review`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's vocabulary strand — precise verbs, figurative language (simile, metaphor, personification, pathetic fallacy, hyperbole, onomatopoeia, idiom), connotation, emotive language, technical vocabulary, and register — into a meta-level skill: choosing the best vocabulary tool for a specific writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Strategic vocabulary selection`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience. This review lesson assesses whether students have moved beyond technique knowledge to genuine technique selection — the highest-level vocabulary skill at Phase 2.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Year grammar review — mixed features from all four terms`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge & Practice): students review the full year's grammar strand — sentence types, conjunctions, tenses, voice, modal verbs, reported speech, conditional sentences, and clauses — applying them in a mixed, integrated context.`,{title:`I Do — One Sentence, Many Grammar Features`,instruction:`By now, a sophisticated sentence can contain multiple grammar features at once. Watch me build one deliberately, naming each feature as I add it.`,example:`Build-up: Base: The scientists made a discovery. Add passive: A discovery was made. Add modal: A significant discovery may have been made. Add relative clause: ... by the scientists, who had been working on the project for over a decade. Add concessive: Although the research had taken over a decade, a significant discovery may finally have been made — one that could change everything.`,demonstration:`Each step adds a grammatical feature: passive voice, modal verb, relative clause, concessive conjunction. The sentence remains grammatically correct throughout. This is the level of control expected heading into Year 7.`,tip:`If you're unsure whether a multi-feature sentence is correct, simplify it. A clear, correct simpler sentence is better than a tangled complex one.`},{title:`We Do — Identify All Features`,instruction:`Identify every grammar feature from the year's learning in this sentence.`,sentences:[`Despite the fact that the match had been postponed three times, the players, who had trained for months, insisted that they would be ready — if the ground was deemed safe.`],prompt:`Concessive clause? Passive voice? Past perfect? Relative clause? Conditional? Modal verb? How many features can we find?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Identify the grammar features in each sentence, then write one original sentence that combines at least three features from the year.`,tasks:[`'I should have been told earlier,' she muttered, even though she suspected she wouldn't have listened.`,`The ancient bridge, which had stood for 400 years, was finally demolished — a decision that the council had debated for over a decade.`,`✦ Bonus: Write three sentences — one from each main text type (narrative, persuasive, explanation) — each containing at least three grammar features from the year. Label each feature.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Year grammar toolkit review`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's grammar strand — sentence types (S/Co/Cx), conjunction types, tense system (simple past, present, past perfect), active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, and clause types (relative, subordinate, concessive, participial) — in an integrated, multi-feature context.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Integrated grammar application`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and structures. This review lesson assesses the highest-level Phase 2 grammatical skill: combining multiple grammatical features within a single, still-correct sentence — the standard expected at Year 7.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Planning and drafting a complete chosen-text-type piece`,`Writing — Composing (Practice) & Writing Process (Knowledge): students independently plan and draft a complete piece in their chosen text type, applying the year's full toolkit — deliberately planned before drafting.`,{title:`I Do — The Full Pre-Draft Plan`,instruction:`Today we write a complete piece. But first — the full plan. Watch me build one for a persuasive piece in 5 minutes, covering all the dimensions we've learned.`,example:`Topic: why every school should have a dedicated nature area. Text type: persuasive. Hook: rhetorical question and statistic. Structure: intro, 3 PEEL paragraphs, counter and rebuttal, conclusion. Vocabulary: emotive language, concessive phrases, precise verbs. Grammar: conditional sentences, relative clauses for definitions. Sentence variety: -ing opener, colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for closing. Register: formal, third person.`,demonstration:`Everything is decided before I write word one. The drafting is now execution, not exploration. This means the draft will be more controlled and better structured from the start.`,tip:`Your plan should be specific enough that someone else could write the piece from it. If it's vague, it won't help you when you're actually drafting.`},{title:`We Do — Plan Together`,instruction:`As a class, build the full plan for a narrative piece about an unexpected discovery.`,sentences:[`Topic: a character finds something buried in their garden that changes everything.`],prompt:`What goes in the plan? Is it specific enough that the draft will write itself from it?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Choose your text type. Build your full plan. Then draft your complete piece (minimum 3–4 paragraphs). Label at least 5 techniques in the draft.`,tasks:[`Choose one: Narrative (a moment of unexpected change) / Persuasive (a cause you believe in) / Explanation (a natural process you understand well).`,`✦ Bonus: After drafting, circle every deliberate technique you used. Were there any in your draft that weren't in your plan? Were there any in your plan that you forgot to include?`]},[{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Independent complete-piece composition`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, demonstrating the full integration of the year's vocabulary, grammar, sentence structure, and text organisation strategies in an independently composed, complete piece of writing.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)`,label:`Planning as part of the writing process`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan systematically before drafting, making deliberate decisions about text type, structure, vocabulary, grammar, and sentence variety that guide and control the drafting process. This lesson demonstrates that planning-before-drafting is the foundation of quality independent writing at Phase 2 and beyond.`}]),Thursday:g(`Full publication-ready edit of own writing`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a comprehensive, sequential editing process (structural, paragraph-level, sentence-level, word-level, final proofread) to the piece they drafted on Wednesday, producing a publication-ready version.`,{title:`I Do — The Five-Pass Edit`,instruction:`Professional writers edit in passes — top down, not randomly. Watch me apply all five passes to a paragraph.`,example:`Five passes: 1. STRUCTURAL — is the whole piece in the right shape? 2. PARAGRAPH — do topic sentences and closers work? 3. SENTENCE — variety, sentence types, clause work. 4. WORD — precise verbs, figurative language, connotation, register. 5. PROOFREAD — punctuation, spelling, grammar errors.`,demonstration:`Pass 1: structure check — nothing to fix. Pass 2: first topic sentence is weak — fix it. Pass 3: two consecutive simple sentences — combine one. Pass 4: 'went' appears twice — replace both. Pass 5: missing comma after fronted adverbial. Five focused passes catch what one frantic reading misses.`,tip:`Do NOT proofread first. Structural problems waste your proofreading effort on sentences you might delete.`},{title:`We Do — Pass by Pass`,instruction:`Together, take a sample paragraph through all five passes in order, making one fix per pass.`,sentences:[`The forest was big and old. Many animals lived there. People were cutting it down and this was bad for the animals. Something should be done about this. It needs to stop.`],prompt:`Pass 1: Does this have the right structure for its type? Pass 2: Topic sentence? Pass 3: Sentence variety? Pass 4: Precise verbs and word choice? Pass 5: Punctuation errors?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Apply all five passes — in order — to the piece you drafted on Wednesday. Mark every change and note which pass caught it.`,tasks:[`Pass 1: Structure check`,`Pass 2: Paragraph-level check`,`Pass 3: Sentence-level check`,`Pass 4: Word-level check`,`Pass 5: Final proofread`,`✦ Bonus: Count how many changes each pass found. Which pass was most productive for your piece? What does that tell you about where you most need to improve as a writer?`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Five-pass sequential editing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking vocabulary, punctuation, and structure. The five-pass sequential edit — structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread — is the publication-standard editing process that consolidates every editing skill developed across the year.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)`,label:`Sequential editing as professional practice`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective editing is sequential — structural problems are addressed before paragraph-level issues, which are addressed before sentence-level issues, which are addressed before word choice, before proofreading. This is the capstone editing lesson of the year.`}])},10:{Monday:g(`Year celebration — vocabulary Hall of Fame`,`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge & Practice): students reflect on and celebrate the most powerful vocabulary tools they have learned across the year, selecting their personal Hall of Fame and articulating why each device is effective.`,{title:`I Do — What Made the Biggest Difference?`,instruction:`We've built a huge vocabulary toolkit this year. Today, I'll share my Hall of Fame — the vocabulary techniques that make the biggest difference to writing quality — and explain exactly why.`,example:`My Hall of Fame nominations: 1. Precise verbs — because one strong verb does what ten weak words can't. 2. Pathetic fallacy — because it makes the whole world part of the story. 3. Connotation — because the difference between 'determined' and 'stubborn' is the difference between a hero and a villain.`,demonstration:`For each, I give a reason that goes beyond 'it sounds better'. The reflection IS the learning.`,tip:`Your Hall of Fame should be personal — the techniques that changed YOUR writing, not just the ones that sound impressive to mention.`},{title:`We Do — Class Hall of Fame`,instruction:`As a class, nominate vocabulary techniques for the class Hall of Fame and make the case for each one.`,sentences:[`Nominations open: which vocabulary technique made the biggest difference to your writing this year? Make the case in one sentence.`],prompt:`Why THIS technique specifically? What does it let a writer do that they couldn't do without it?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Write your personal vocabulary Hall of Fame — three techniques, with one paragraph of justification for each.`,tasks:[`Choice 1 and why it matters.`,`Choice 2 and why it matters.`,`Choice 3 and why it matters.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph that deliberately uses all three of your Hall of Fame techniques. Annotate it so a reader could see exactly where each one appears and what it does.`]},[{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)`,label:`Metacognitive vocabulary reflection`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Synthesises the year's vocabulary strand through metacognitive reflection — students evaluate which vocabulary tools have been most effective for their own writing development and articulate the reason, demonstrating the highest-level understanding: not just knowing a technique, but understanding why it works.`},{code:`Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)`,label:`Personal vocabulary Hall of Fame`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students demonstrate ownership of vocabulary skills by selecting, justifying, and applying their most personally significant vocabulary techniques. This culminating activity confirms that vocabulary knowledge has become internalised and transferable — the standard expected at the transition to Year 7.`}]),Tuesday:g(`Year celebration — grammar mastery challenge`,`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge & Practice): students complete a mixed grammar mastery challenge drawing on the full year's grammar strand, demonstrating secure knowledge of all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts.`,{title:`I Do — The Grammar Full Circuit`,instruction:`Today we go back to the beginning and run the full grammar circuit — every major concept from all four terms. Watch me work through a mixed challenge, naming each feature as I go.`,example:`Challenge: rewrite this passage to include a complex sentence, past perfect flashback, reported speech, passive voice, modal verb, and concessive clause.`,demonstration:`Original: The team won. The captain was happy. She said she was proud. → Rewrite: Although the season had been difficult — they had nearly given up after a losing streak early on — the team finally won the championship. The captain was reported to have said that she was overwhelmingly proud. It was a result that should have come sooner.`,tip:`Work feature by feature through the challenge list, not all at once. Tick each one off before moving to the next.`},{title:`We Do — Mixed Challenge`,instruction:`Complete this grammar challenge together — one feature at a time.`,sentences:[`Base sentence: The school held a special event. It was a surprise. Everyone enjoyed it. Challenge: add a relative clause, a past perfect, a modal verb, and a concessive clause.`],prompt:`Which feature do we add first? Does the sentence remain grammatically correct after each addition?`},{title:`You Do — Your Turn`,instruction:`Complete the grammar mastery challenge. Rewrite the passage using ALL the grammar features listed.`,tasks:[`Base: Scientists found a new species. They had worked for years. The discovery was important. Required features: passive voice, past perfect, modal verb, relative clause, concessive clause, reported speech.`,`✦ Bonus: Write a fully original paragraph — topic of your choice — that contains every grammar feature from the list above, correctly used. Annotate each one.`]},[{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)`,label:`Year grammar mastery challenge`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The year's grammar mastery challenge synthesises all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts — sentence types, conjunctions, tense system, voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, concessive clauses, and clause types — in a single integrated task that confirms secure grammatical understanding heading into Year 7.`},{code:`Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)`,label:`Grammar integration at Year 7 readiness level`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students demonstrate that they can write grammatically correct sentences using the full range of Phase 2 structures simultaneously — the standard expected for Year 7 transition.`}]),Wednesday:g(`Year celebration — flexible writing showcase`,`Writing — Composing (Practice): students write a short, polished piece in their chosen text type on a topic of their choice, showcasing the full range of the year's techniques as a celebration of writing growth.`,{title:`I Do — Writing with Everything You Know`,instruction:`Today is a free write — but 'free' doesn't mean unplanned. It means you choose the topic, the text type, and the techniques. You decide what this piece is and what it does. Watch me make those decisions confidently and fast.`,example:`My choice: narrative. Topic: the last day of summer. Techniques: pathetic fallacy for the opening, precise verbs throughout, one deliberate fragment, and a colon for the emotional revelation in the final paragraph. Register: reflective, first person. Length: 4 paragraphs.`,demonstration:`I commit to a plan in 2 minutes, then draft with confidence. The decisions are fast because I trust my toolkit. That's what a year of writing practice is supposed to produce.`,tip:`Don't spend more than 3 minutes planning. Make confident choices and write. The editing pass comes afterwards.`},{title:`We Do — Share Plans`,instruction:`Each student shares their plan (text type, topic, 3 technique choices) with a partner before writing. Partners give a 30-second response: does the plan sound like it will produce a strong piece?`,sentences:[`Share: text type, topic, three technique choices, and your intended opening hook.`],prompt:`Does the plan hang together? Does the hook suit the text type and topic?`},{title:`You Do — Write Your Showcase Piece`,instruction:`Plan (3 minutes), draft (15 minutes), quick self-edit (5 minutes). Aim for 3–5 paragraphs.`,tasks:[`Choose your text type and topic freely — any text type, any subject that genuinely interests you.`,`Aim to use at least 5 techniques from across the year's learning.`,`After drafting, do a quick 5-minute self-edit — one pass for the most important fix.`,`✦ Bonus: Annotate your showcase piece — circle and label every technique you used deliberately. This is your record of the year's learning in action.`]},[{code:`Writing — Composing (Practice)`,label:`Independent flexible writing showcase`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students independently compose a complete, polished piece in their chosen text type, demonstrating the full range of Phase 2 composing strategies — including planning, technique selection, deliberate vocabulary and grammar choices, and self-editing — without teacher scaffolding. This is the summative composing task for the year.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Planning to publication independently`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students independently manage the full writing process — planning, drafting, and self-editing — with confident, fast decision-making that reflects internalised Phase 2 writing knowledge. This independent control of the writing process is the central goal of the year's programme.`}]),Thursday:g(`Portfolio piece — the whole-year best edit`,`Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students select their best piece of writing from across the year, apply the five-pass edit to bring it to portfolio quality, and write a brief reflection on their growth as a writer.`,{title:`I Do — What Makes a Portfolio Piece?`,instruction:`A portfolio piece is the best version of your best work. Today I'll show you how to choose it and how to bring it to the highest standard it can reach.`,example:`What to look for when choosing: the piece that has the most interesting ideas. The piece where you took the most risks. The piece that is closest to being great — not already perfect, but closest to having genuine power.`,demonstration:`The best portfolio piece is often not the longest or the most technically polished — it's the one with the strongest ideas and the most personal voice. Apply the five-pass edit to bring the execution up to the level of the ideas.`,tip:`Edit the ideas first (structural pass), then everything else. A beautifully proofread piece with weak ideas is still a weak piece.`},{title:`We Do — Select and Plan the Edit`,instruction:`As a class, share: which piece are you choosing for your portfolio and why? What does the structural pass show needs fixing first?`,sentences:[`Share: title of your chosen piece, reason for choosing it, and one structural improvement you know it needs.`],prompt:`Is the reason for choosing it about ideas and voice, not just length or neatness? What is the most important fix?`},{title:`You Do — Final Five-Pass Edit and Reflection`,instruction:`Apply the five-pass edit to your chosen portfolio piece. Then write a 100-150 word reflection.`,tasks:[`Five-pass edit: structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread.`,`Reflection: What was your writing like at the beginning of the year? What can you do now that you couldn't do then? What technique made the biggest difference? What do you want to get better at in Year 7?`,`✦ This is your final writing task for the year. Make it the best version of your best work.`]},[{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Practice)`,label:`Portfolio-quality editing`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students select, revise, and edit a piece to portfolio standard, applying the year's full editing sequence and reflecting metacognitively on their growth. This is the capstone task of the Year 5–6 writing programme.`},{code:`Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)`,label:`Metacognitive reflection on writing growth`,detail:`NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Students reflect on their development as writers, identifying which strategies have most improved their writing and setting goals for future development. This final lesson closes the year's writing programme by consolidating not just skills but self-awareness — the foundation for continued growth as a writer into Year 7 and beyond.`}])}};
